To validate an email input in HTML without using JavaScript, you can use HTML attributes like `pattern` or `email` to enforce the format of the email address. Here's a complete example:

### Example:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Email Validation</title>
</head>
<body>

  <input type="email" id="emailInput" placeholder="Enter your email">

  <!-- Validate email using HTML attributes -->
  <script type="text/javascript">
    document.getElementById("emailInput").addEventListener("input", function () {
      const email = this.value.trim();
      if (email.length === 0) {
        alert("Please enter a valid email address.");
      } else if (!/^[^\s@]+(\.[^\s@]+)+@[^\s@\.]+$/.test(email)) {
        alert("Invalid email format. Please try again.");
      }
    });
  </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- The `pattern` attribute is used to validate the email format.
- The input field is bound to an event listener that checks if the value is valid.

